00:10:03.636 --> 00:10:04.837
With the guillotine that I know,

00:10:04.837 --> 00:10:06.272
you can either get him in here,

00:10:06.272 --> 00:10:09.241
sometimes you can get the
person's neck underneath here

00:10:09.241 --> 00:10:10.443
and just kind of use your hips.

00:10:10.443 --> 00:10:12.345
It's more of a hip position.

00:10:12.345 --> 00:10:13.646
He had him against the octagon,

00:10:13.646 --> 00:10:15.348
so it's kind of hard to get your hips up

00:10:15.348 --> 00:10:17.083
as someone's pressing into you.

00:10:17.083 --> 00:10:19.452
So it leaves you to
focus more on your hands.

00:10:19.452 --> 00:10:21.854
So that looks like a
pretty realistic position.

00:10:25.691 --> 00:10:27.793
He's way too small to pull off that move.

00:10:27.793 --> 00:10:29.328
If someone has your neck here,

00:10:29.328 --> 00:10:32.465
that's the natural defense,
to kind of put your hand over.

00:10:32.465 --> 00:10:35.534
So naturally what you
have to do is go lower

00:10:35.534 --> 00:10:37.470
to let the person roll underneath you,

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:40.172
but it's hard to pick the person up.

00:10:42.942 --> 00:10:44.577
So, the ground stuff looks pretty good.

00:10:44.577 --> 00:10:45.611
A little dramatic.

00:10:45.611 --> 00:10:47.980
If he's turning around
to get that arm bar,

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:49.281
he's stepping around like that.

00:10:49.281 --> 00:10:50.616
That's too much space.

00:10:50.616 --> 00:10:53.119
That person could just
rip their arm out easily.

00:10:53.119 --> 00:10:55.755
Because in grappling, you
gotta stay tight, very close.

00:10:55.755 --> 00:10:57.657
You know, there's no, none of this.

00:10:57.657 --> 00:10:59.825
The thing that I notice a
lot with mixed martial arts

00:10:59.825 --> 00:11:00.860
or martial arts films,

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:02.595
they want that big dramatic effect.

00:11:02.595 --> 00:11:04.163
"Oh, my God, this is happening!"

00:11:10.436 --> 00:11:12.304
So, that's a really good knee bar.

00:11:12.304 --> 00:11:13.839
I mean, it's very deep.

00:11:13.839 --> 00:11:15.508
He's got both legs around.

00:11:15.508 --> 00:11:17.410
He's got the person's
leg close to his head.

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:18.310
That's perfect.

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:19.879
It's dramatic how he's putting his hands

00:11:19.879 --> 00:11:20.913
all the way behind him.

00:11:20.913 --> 00:11:22.948
That's just taking way too much energy.

00:11:22.948 --> 00:11:24.183
All you gotta do is just close your hand

00:11:24.183 --> 00:11:26.285
and press your hip forward.

00:11:26.285 --> 00:11:28.854
I would definitely give
this a 8 out of 10.

00:11:28.854 --> 00:11:31.557
I think it's a great
introduction towards MMA

00:11:31.557 --> 00:11:33.059
and a great story as well.

00:11:38.097 --> 00:11:39.965
I really love Jean-Claude Van Damme.

00:11:39.965 --> 00:11:42.668
He's definitely one of
the guys, if not the guy,

00:11:42.668 --> 00:11:45.337
to inspire me to kick. Because,
you know, I'm a kicker.

00:11:45.337 --> 00:11:46.906
I've knocked out guys with spinning kicks.

00:11:46.906 --> 00:11:48.541
I've learned other cool kicks.

00:11:48.541 --> 00:11:51.644
When I was 12, this was life.

00:11:51.644 --> 00:11:53.479
Round kick's one of my favorite kicks.

00:11:53.479 --> 00:11:54.680
That was pretty accurate.

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:56.749
There are so many ways to do round kicks.

00:11:56.749 --> 00:11:58.918
If you're using the shin part of your leg,

00:11:58.918 --> 00:12:01.487
I would recommend it
to you to chop the leg

00:12:01.487 --> 00:12:03.723
or hit him in the body.

00:12:03.723 --> 00:12:06.492
You can hit him in the face
a little harder. Use shin.

00:12:09.195 --> 00:12:10.796
H--- no, this will never work.

00:12:10.796 --> 00:12:12.665
Nobody should ever stand there

00:12:12.665 --> 00:12:15.601
and get kicked with the same kick.

00:12:15.601 --> 00:12:17.336
Put your hands up, champ!

00:12:17.336 --> 00:12:19.305
In a real fight, I would definitely

00:12:19.305 --> 00:12:21.107
be throwing a lot of low kicks.

00:12:21.107 --> 00:12:23.442
As a fighter, I think more like a sniper.

00:12:23.442 --> 00:12:26.312
I'm the guy that waits, and
I look for that perfect shot,

00:12:26.312 --> 00:12:29.014
and I take it. And I'm
very precise with it.

00:12:29.014 --> 00:12:31.117
I contribute that to my karate background,

00:12:31.117 --> 00:12:34.720
because in karate, we
had to do tornado kicks

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:36.922
and kick apples off each other's heads.

00:12:36.922 --> 00:12:39.358
So that's the type of
accuracy I had to have,

00:12:39.358 --> 00:12:42.962
and it helped me throughout
the rest of my career.

00:12:46.232 --> 00:12:48.134
Jean-Claude Van Damme is the only dude

00:12:48.134 --> 00:12:50.035
that punches and flex.

00:12:50.035 --> 00:12:52.104
"Ah!"

00:12:52.104 --> 00:12:54.140
And you just looking at the muscle.

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:55.641
You're not even looking at the move!

00:13:01.647 --> 00:13:03.516
That's where I got that kick from.

00:13:03.516 --> 00:13:05.684
The spinning hook kick.

00:13:05.684 --> 00:13:07.419
So many ways to do this kick.

00:13:07.419 --> 00:13:09.455
What Jean-Claude Van
Damme just did right there

00:13:09.455 --> 00:13:11.290
is an extension of his leg.

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:13.325
The further that leg is out,

00:13:13.325 --> 00:13:15.861
the more whip it's going to have,

00:13:15.861 --> 00:13:17.763
the stronger it's going to be.

00:13:17.763 --> 00:13:19.732
When I did this kick to
knock out my opponent

00:13:19.732 --> 00:13:22.668
on "The Ultimate Fighter,"
I chambered my leg.

00:13:25.004 --> 00:13:26.305
So I spun,

00:13:26.305 --> 00:13:29.475
but instead of extending
it, I kept it intact.

00:13:29.475 --> 00:13:31.911
Now, that was because of the distance,

00:13:31.911 --> 00:13:34.346
because if he's close and I extend my leg,

00:13:34.346 --> 00:13:36.215
it's going to create that off balance.

00:13:36.215 --> 00:13:38.184
But in that split of a second,

00:13:38.184 --> 00:13:40.452
you have to recognize that distance.

00:13:41.921 --> 00:13:43.022
Oh!

00:13:43.889 --> 00:13:45.524
Pew!

00:13:45.524 --> 00:13:49.495
I've been attempting that kick for years.

00:13:49.495 --> 00:13:50.529
Still haven't gotten it.

00:13:50.529 --> 00:13:52.464
You gotta be able to have
a good split to do that,

00:13:52.464 --> 00:13:55.167
because what happened is
when you extend that leg,

00:13:55.167 --> 00:13:57.136
you're using the other leg as momentum

00:13:57.136 --> 00:13:59.004
to generate that power.

00:13:59.004 --> 00:14:01.106
Not as realistic as it seemed,

00:14:01.106 --> 00:14:03.709
because it's so much
energy to generate that.

00:14:03.709 --> 00:14:06.579
Takes time to throw it, and in that time,

00:14:06.579 --> 00:14:09.982
that person could have a
defense mechanism to go,

00:14:09.982 --> 00:14:10.883
"I'm outta here."

00:14:14.086 --> 00:14:16.689
A lot of people don't train
this area on your leg.

00:14:16.689 --> 00:14:18.591
You know, it's that soft meat, your calf,

00:14:18.591 --> 00:14:20.426
or even right below your ankle.

00:14:20.426 --> 00:14:21.727
It's like hitting with a bat.

00:14:21.727 --> 00:14:24.730
So I absolutely think leg
kicks can win a fight.

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:27.566
A great movie, but it's not even close.

00:14:27.566 --> 00:14:28.868
So I'ma give it a 5.

00:14:28.868 --> 00:14:31.170
Sorry, bro. Still love you.

00:14:40.613 --> 00:14:42.448
As you see, Keanu is just evading,

00:14:42.448 --> 00:14:44.416
and that's one of the key things too.

00:14:44.416 --> 00:14:46.685
If someone's either sporadic or wild

00:14:46.685 --> 00:14:49.822
or they're just throwing,
it's good to kind of evade.

00:14:49.822 --> 00:14:51.290
I was taught a long time ago,

00:14:51.290 --> 00:14:53.459
you can never fight with rage.

00:14:53.459 --> 00:14:54.493
It just blinds you.

00:14:54.493 --> 00:14:56.595
I've done it once in my
career, and it cost me,

00:14:56.595 --> 00:14:58.264
and I can say that plays a part

00:14:58.264 --> 00:14:59.932
in her just attack, attack, attack.

00:14:59.932 --> 00:15:00.866
There's the opening.

00:15:00.866 --> 00:15:02.935
And he blocked and defended it.

00:15:02.935 --> 00:15:06.305
He grabbed ahold of her
and gave her a judo throw.

00:15:06.305 --> 00:15:08.507
It's genius, where someone
keeps coming at you,

00:15:08.507 --> 00:15:10.709
you just use their momentum to throw them.

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:15.814
Yeah, he's holding her back,

00:15:15.814 --> 00:15:18.517
but she could've easily
kicked him, throw some knees.

00:15:18.517 --> 00:15:20.586
She has her legs. She
wasn't really using that.

00:15:23.989 --> 00:15:26.191
There is no pause in a
real fight like that.

00:15:26.191 --> 00:15:28.193
I wouldn't be like, "You OK?"

00:15:28.193 --> 00:15:29.795
You know, you grab it, you break.

00:15:29.795 --> 00:15:32.731
But other than that, you know,
this was just phenomenal.

00:15:32.731 --> 00:15:35.501
Credit to Keanu for
doing all of his stunts.

00:15:35.501 --> 00:15:37.002
I would give it a 10 out of 10.

00:15:37.036 --> 00:15:38.637
Hi, my name's Miriam Nakamoto.

00:15:38.637 --> 00:15:40.606
I'm an eight-time Muay
Thai world champion,

00:15:40.606 --> 00:15:43.008
and I've been doing it
for over two decades.

00:15:43.008 --> 00:15:44.743
Today we're going to be
looking at Muay Thai scenes

00:15:44.743 --> 00:15:46.312
from movies and TV,

00:15:50.115 --> 00:15:51.417
His teep is beautiful.

00:15:51.417 --> 00:15:53.819
The teep is a foot jab.

00:15:53.819 --> 00:15:56.755
You can see it starts from his back leg,

00:15:56.755 --> 00:15:59.391
comes up his body, and goes, pat,

00:15:59.391 --> 00:16:01.393
right out through the lead leg.

00:16:01.393 --> 00:16:02.995
That would be the position you'd be in

00:16:02.995 --> 00:16:03.996
in a Muay Thai fight.

00:16:03.996 --> 00:16:05.664
But, yeah, a teep is
good if somebody keeps

00:16:05.664 --> 00:16:08.667
coming in on you and
you want to get space.

00:16:08.667 --> 00:16:11.170
A high kick to the head looks cool,

00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:13.906
and if you have a nice head
kick, it's a great knockout.

00:16:13.906 --> 00:16:16.575
But for me, I prefer to kick to the body.

00:16:16.575 --> 00:16:19.845
With the head kick, if you
hit with the top of your foot

00:16:19.845 --> 00:16:22.614
to somebody's skull, yes, it hurts them,

00:16:22.614 --> 00:16:24.316
but, yes, it hurts you too.

00:16:24.316 --> 00:16:26.986
And I'm not trying to be
limping around, you know,

00:16:26.986 --> 00:16:30.122
with a messed-up foot for
a month after that fight.

00:16:31.957 --> 00:16:35.327
For Muay Thai, I've
never seen that stance.

00:16:35.327 --> 00:16:38.364
Because he was really,
really low to the ground.

00:16:38.364 --> 00:16:39.331
It's never that low.

00:16:39.331 --> 00:16:41.400
I guess that's Boran.

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:45.604
And so they do more of that,
and I'm just more straight up.

00:16:45.604 --> 00:16:47.806
The logic in Muay Thai
of standing taller is

00:16:47.806 --> 00:16:48.774
as you're getting kicked

00:16:48.774 --> 00:16:50.476
and as you want access to your kicks,

00:16:50.476 --> 00:16:52.344
the lower you are, the
longer it's going to take

00:16:52.344 --> 00:16:53.846
to get your leg up there.

00:16:53.846 --> 00:16:55.748
But if you're already standing taller,

00:16:55.748 --> 00:16:58.017
you can just rotate your
body, and it's there.

00:16:59.752 --> 00:17:03.555
If you can catch their chin,
yes, it's a short night.

00:17:03.555 --> 00:17:05.024
I've had a lot of knee surgeries.

00:17:05.024 --> 00:17:07.626
Probably not in the cards
for me, unfortunately.

00:17:07.626 --> 00:17:10.596
But what I find is awesome about knees

00:17:10.596 --> 00:17:14.833
is when you're lighting somebody
up with knees to the body,

00:17:14.833 --> 00:17:16.769
it actually takes the fight out of them,

00:17:16.769 --> 00:17:18.504
kind of tenderizes the meat.

00:17:18.504 --> 00:17:21.407
And his form is perfect in his knees.

00:17:21.407 --> 00:17:25.411
By rotating the femur in, then
your lower half of your leg

00:17:25.411 --> 00:17:28.147
and your foot goes kind of outward.

00:17:28.147 --> 00:17:29.348
Then you point your toes,

00:17:29.348 --> 00:17:31.283
and it creates a much more lancing effect.

00:17:31.283 --> 00:17:33.652
And you can also get more
range on your knee too,

00:17:33.652 --> 00:17:35.487
which keeps you further away from danger

00:17:35.487 --> 00:17:37.990
as you're implementing damage.

00:17:39.858 --> 00:17:42.661
So, those punches were terrible,
but it's a stunt scene.

00:17:42.661 --> 00:17:44.430
I did one stunt scene, and I was like,

00:17:44.430 --> 00:17:47.299
"Oh, this is not even real at all."

00:17:47.299 --> 00:17:49.668
For a film, you'd want
to take it out and around

00:17:49.668 --> 00:17:51.770
so you can really see it and sell it.

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:54.006
But when you're actually fighting,

00:17:54.006 --> 00:17:55.908
you want to just keep
it tight to your body.

00:17:55.908 --> 00:17:56.842
Don't want to lift your elbow.

00:17:56.842 --> 00:17:59.578
Just want to rotate, just disguise it.

00:17:59.578 --> 00:18:01.780
Because the more you have a tell,

00:18:01.780 --> 00:18:03.015
the more your opponent sees it

00:18:03.015 --> 00:18:04.450
and they can prepare for that.

00:18:07.086 --> 00:18:09.121
That is accurate, the knee shield,

00:18:09.121 --> 00:18:11.256
keeping somebody off you.

00:18:11.256 --> 00:18:14.293
That is definitely a
legitimate thing to get space.

00:18:19.798 --> 00:18:21.300
I firmly believe he can jump up there

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:23.135
and put both his knees like that.

00:18:23.135 --> 00:18:26.538
But, yeah, that's not
happening in a fight.

00:18:26.538 --> 00:18:27.539
That's too much of a gamble.

00:18:27.539 --> 00:18:29.241
I mean, you're spending
a lot of time in air.

00:18:29.241 --> 00:18:33.245
You could stay on the ground
and just drive forward

00:18:33.245 --> 00:18:35.747
and slam an elbow into their face.

00:18:39.685 --> 00:18:41.987
And they bow. Yeah, that's accurate too.

00:18:41.987 --> 00:18:44.756
Always bow to an opponent,
whether you win or you lose.

00:18:44.756 --> 00:18:48.260
I have to rate it high,
because it's Tony Jaa.

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:50.129
So I have to say 9 out of 10.

00:18:55.601 --> 00:18:57.536
Her form is great on those elbows.

00:18:57.536 --> 00:19:01.373
So, elbows is the shortest-range weapon

00:19:01.373 --> 00:19:02.808
of a Muay Thai fight.

00:19:02.808 --> 00:19:04.643
There's a up elbow,

00:19:05.611 --> 00:19:08.380
and then there's round elbows.

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:10.082
And then there's a Tomahawk elbow,

00:19:10.082 --> 00:19:12.384
and then there's spike elbows too.

00:19:12.384 --> 00:19:14.653
So what she's doing in this
scene is a spike elbow.

00:19:14.653 --> 00:19:15.687
In a Muay Thai fight,

00:19:15.687 --> 00:19:18.123
I would not elbow anywhere but the head.

00:19:18.123 --> 00:19:19.691
Because if you're elbowing somewhere else,

00:19:19.691 --> 00:19:22.227
that means your head is down,

00:19:22.227 --> 00:19:24.163
where there's a lot of danger.

00:19:24.163 --> 00:19:26.265
So that was very, very, very realistic.

00:19:29.034 --> 00:19:31.036
That, no. That's not Muay Thai.

00:19:31.036 --> 00:19:33.705
What Muay Thai is, is
you take a boxing match,

00:19:33.705 --> 00:19:36.341
and then you add not only punches,

00:19:36.341 --> 00:19:37.743
but you add in kicks.

00:19:37.743 --> 00:19:41.713
And not only kicks, you can also elbow,

00:19:41.713 --> 00:19:44.383
knee, and standing grappling.

00:19:47.886 --> 00:19:50.155
That kick is awesome.
I need to learn that.

00:19:50.155 --> 00:19:53.592
The spinning kick looked
correct. It's not common.

00:19:53.592 --> 00:19:56.495
You wouldn't see that very
often in a Muay Thai fight.

00:19:56.495 --> 00:19:59.464
Usually you just see round
kick, you see the foot jabs,

00:19:59.464 --> 00:20:01.833
you see the low kick,
a question-mark kick,

00:20:01.833 --> 00:20:04.870
where it comes up here
and then it goes around.

00:20:04.870 --> 00:20:05.904
Well, I'll say 8 out of 10,

00:20:05.904 --> 00:20:08.774
just 'cause that whole jumping
up on the shoulders thing

00:20:08.774 --> 00:20:10.209
and flinging yourself down is like,

00:20:10.209 --> 00:20:11.043
no, that's not gonna happen.

00:20:11.043 --> 00:20:12.311
But everything else looked great.

00:20:15.881 --> 00:20:19.384
Yes, foot stomp. Heck yeah,
definitely do those knees.

00:20:19.384 --> 00:20:21.286
The kick, not as much.

00:20:21.286 --> 00:20:23.055
I don't know why you would be kicking

00:20:23.055 --> 00:20:24.590
in that really small space.

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:27.292
There's a lot better things you could use.

00:20:27.292 --> 00:20:31.363
I would do punches, elbows, and knees,

00:20:31.363 --> 00:20:33.865
because those are the
more close-range weapons.

00:20:33.865 --> 00:20:36.001
And then the push kick would be good too,

00:20:36.001 --> 00:20:37.369
because they're in front of you.

00:20:37.369 --> 00:20:39.938
You wouldn't really do
round kicks and high kicks,

00:20:39.938 --> 00:20:41.773
because you have to go out and around.

00:20:41.773 --> 00:20:45.010
Like, yeah, no, you want
it to just go this way.

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:48.914
He actually blocked a kick
with his hand, forearm.

00:20:48.914 --> 00:20:50.215
Don't do that at home, folks.

00:20:50.215 --> 00:20:52.784
Hand or forearm against shin,

00:20:52.784 --> 00:20:55.320
like, look at this, who wins?

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:57.356
You can get your arm broken,
get your hand broken.

00:20:59.858 --> 00:21:03.629
Well, I like that he keeps
trying to cover and block.

00:21:03.629 --> 00:21:06.598
That would be definitely
what you want to do.

00:21:06.598 --> 00:21:08.533
I mean, the hands are kind
of all over the place.

00:21:08.533 --> 00:21:10.402
Yeah, you don't wanna be ...

00:21:10.402 --> 00:21:11.470
that's not good.

00:21:11.470 --> 00:21:13.572
If somebody's striking at you,

00:21:13.572 --> 00:21:15.173
you just need your hands up.

00:21:15.173 --> 00:21:17.242
I would give it a 6,
because it's basically

00:21:17.242 --> 00:21:18.877
if you have a hallway in your house

00:21:18.877 --> 00:21:20.579
and you're going to
have a full-blown fight.

00:21:20.579 --> 00:21:22.881
My name is Shifu Yanzi.

00:21:22.881 --> 00:21:27.219
Since 1983, I went to Shaolin Temple,

00:21:27.219 --> 00:21:30.022
so now I'm the 34th-generation

00:21:30.022 --> 00:21:33.525
Shaolin kung fu warrior, disciple.

00:21:33.525 --> 00:21:37.329
My Shaolin experience
is more than 35 years.

00:21:37.329 --> 00:21:40.465
Today I will break down
some kung fu movies.

00:21:45.137 --> 00:21:47.639
He come here and try to fly-jump at him,

00:21:47.639 --> 00:21:51.777
then he straightaway know, he
don't need some fancy move.

00:21:51.777 --> 00:21:53.345
He just lies down on the floor.

00:21:53.345 --> 00:21:56.548
He give you one throw and give
you one kick. It's simple.

